EUCLIDEAN HALF-POLYGONS — halving every regular n-gon that can be halved     2026-08-14, CC
================================================================================================

THE FAMILY IS FINITE, AND SMALL. A regular n-gon has a vertex-to-vertex long diagonal and a
midpoint-to-midpoint cut when n is even, and one vertex-to-opposite-midpoint mirror when n is odd.
That is a board per (n, cut) — infinitely many candidates, of which only a handful can hold a tiling.

  FIRST FILTER, THE ANGLES. A vertex figure needs the half's corners to compose 360 exactly, and every
  corner class needs to appear in some composition. For the VERTEX cut the corners are alpha/2 and
  alpha with alpha = 180(n-2)/n, so a*(alpha/2) + b*alpha = 360 means
        a + 2b = 720/alpha = 4n/(n-2) = 4 + 8/(n-2),
  a whole number only when (n-2) divides 8: n in {3,4,6,10}, and 3 has no vertex-to-vertex diagonal.
  The midpoint cut resolves the same way to n in {4,6,8}, the odd mirror to n in {3,5}. Eight boards.
  Everything else dies before a search runs — the half-dodecagon's 75 and 150 degree corners compose
  360 in no combination at all, and neither do the half-octagon's 67.5 and 135.

  SECOND FILTER, THE EDGE SLOTS, and it kills two of the eight. Angles are not the whole rule: the
  edges have to match too. Each corner at a vertex contributes TWO edge-slots (its two flanks) and each
  edge at that vertex is shared by exactly TWO corners, so for every edge TYPE the slot count must be
  EVEN. That is a parity condition on the edges, the flat cousin of the full/half alternation that
  empties {3,7} in H2.
    n=10 vertex — each 72-degree corner is flanked by one unit edge and the long DIAGONAL, and no other
      corner touches the diagonal at all, so the diagonal-slot count is the number of 72-degree corners
      a. The angle equation forces a + 2b = 5, so a is always ODD. No vertex figure exists — gen_alphabet
      returns an alphabet of ZERO entries, which is this fact and not a bug.
    n=8 midpoint — the CUT is flanked only by the two 90-degree corners, so cut-slots = the number of
      90s, c. From 90c + 135b = 360 the only options are (c,b) = (4,0) and (1,2), and c must be even, so
      only four right angles survives. But then NO vertex can ever host a 135-degree corner, and every
      tile has four of them. The alphabet has exactly 1 vertex configuration and the search returns 0.
  Re-derived independently (a from-scratch enumeration of edge-matched cyclic vertex figures, sharing no
  code with gen_alphabet) over n = 3..20: LIVE for exactly n=3 mirror, n=4 vertex, n=4 midpoint,
  n=5 mirror, n=6 vertex, n=6 midpoint. Every board from n=7 up is empty, and always the same way — the
  alpha and alpha/2 corners become unplaceable and only the right angle survives.

  SO THE WHOLE EUCLIDEAN FAMILY IS SIX BOARDS. Two were already shipped. Four are new.

