  n=4 diagonal   45-45-90 triangle      the tri45 shelf (2026-08-13), [6,3,3] at D=24, edges S/H

THE SIX RUN HERE
  eu-half-sq-mid   D=4   [1,1,1,1]        1,2,1,2                the DOMINO (square, midpoint cut)
  eu-half-pent     D=20  [3,6,6,5]        2,2,1,c1+c3            pentagon, mirror
  eu-half-hex-v    D=6   [1,2,2,1]        1,1,1,2                hexagon, long diagonal (trapezoid)
  eu-half-hex-m    D=12  [3,4,4,4,3]      1,2,2,1,2sqrt3         hexagon, midpoint cut
  eu-half-oct-m    D=8   [2,3,3,3,3,2]    1,2,2,2,1,2+2sqrt2     octagon, midpoint cut
  eu-half-dec-v    D=10  [2,4,4,4,4,2]    1,1,1,1,1,2phi         decagon, long diagonal

  Every one closes to 1e-15 as a flat polygon under the developer's edge convention (edgeLens[i] is
  the side from vertex i to vertex i+1 — checked, not assumed, by walking the boundary).

ONE TOOL CHANGE: A GENERAL CHORD TOKEN
  The half-pentagon's cut is the pentagon's HEIGHT, R + apothem = cot(18 deg) = sqrt(5 + 2 sqrt5) =
  3.077683537175254. It is an algebraic integer of Z[zeta20], so the tile is representable — but it is
  not sqrt2, sqrt3, sqrt6 or phi, which is all develop_tri45's length grammar knew. It IS the chord sum
  2cos(18) + 2cos(54). So surd_offsets grew `c<j>` = zeta^j + zeta^-j for any j, which is the general
  case the four named surds were always instances of: verified sqrt2 == c(D/8), sqrt3 == c(D/12),
  phi == c(D/10) give identical offset tuples, and every existing length string parses unchanged.
  (I first told AL this tile needed a 2sqrt5 scale. Wrong: I had measured the APOTHEM instead of the
  height. At scale x2 the cut is an algebraic integer and no exotic scale is needed; what it needed was
  a way to WRITE it.)

