      nothing at k = 1, 3, 4, 5, 6.
  eu-half-sq-mid  square, midpoint            1x2 rectangle (the DOMINO)         1 tiling, at k=1
      nothing at k = 2..6, and that one is PROVABLY the only one. Every corner is a right angle flanked
      by one long and one short side, so the four edges at a vertex alternate long/short; two edges
      separated by one other sit at 180 degrees, hence collinear; so the long edges lie on one family of
      parallel lines and the short edges on the perpendicular family, which is the aligned 1x2 grid and
      nothing else. Running bond, herringbone and basketweave are all T-junction patterns and are not
      edge-to-edge, which is why they are absent and not missing.
  eu-half-oct-m   octagon, midpoint           0 — proved empty above
  eu-half-dec-v   decagon, long diagonal      0 — proved empty above (alphabet has zero entries)

  TOTAL 27,728 Euclidean half-polygon tilings, ZERO develop failures on any board.

INDEPENDENT CHECK — the cells actually tile
  develop refuses anything that does not close, but it never compares a cell's contents to the cell.
  So, on all 27,728 records: the shoelace areas of a fundamental cell's faces must sum to |det(T1,T2)|
  (too little is a gap, too much an overlap), and every face's side and angle multisets must be the
  tile's. 0 area mismatches, 0 wrong-shape faces, worst area error 3.15e-08 — which is the 9-decimal
  rounding of the shipped float coordinates, the exact Z[zeta_D] values sitting behind them.

WHAT IS NOT DONE
  These are enumerated and checked, not shelved. k=13 (hex-v) and k=14 (pent) are BUDGETS, not
  enumeration results: both boards were still growing when the runs stopped.

ON THE SHELF (2026-08-14, later)
  Library -> Euclidean -> Different edge lengths -> Palette. FOUR FOLDERS, one per TILE SHAPE, beside the
  three that were already there:
      Planigons | 45-45-90 triangles and squares | Penrose kite and dart
      Half hexagon (long diagonal) | Half pentagon | Half hexagon (edge midpoints) | Domino (half square)
  One chip per shape and not one for the whole family: a trapezoid, a quadrilateral, a pentagon and a
  domino behind a single "Halved regular polygons" word is not a choice a visitor can make. Two more
  members of the family are already in that list under their own names — the half triangle is one of the
  planigons, the half square IS the 45-45-90 board — which is the whole six-board family on one screen.

  scripts/build-euhalf-shelf.mjs re-certifies against the bytes that ship, not the developer's scratch
  file: every face congruent to the tile, faces covering the period cell exactly, and every vertex a full
  turn. All 27,728 pass, and all 27,728 are already distinct up to congruence — the exact oracle
  (rotations by 2pi/D and a real reflection, lattice carried onto lattice, every tile matched) merged
  NOTHING, so the pruner had already done its job. The oracle is self-checked first: each sampled entry
  is rotated, reflected and shifted off its own lattice and must come back congruent to itself, because a
  predicate that always answered "different" would report a clean dedup too.

  ⚑ MY VERTEX KEY WAS THE BUG, NOT THE DATA. The angle certificate first reported a 660-degree vertex on
  the trapezoid board. I had keyed vertices by their FRACTIONAL coordinates through a wrap function that
  returns 0 for one sign and -0 for the other, so a vertex sitting at fraction ~0 landed in two different
  keys and unrelated points merged. With build-tri45-shelf's residue (reduce to [0,1) in the lattice
  basis, nudge the near-1 seam, key in CARTESIAN, normalise -0) all 27,159 records are clean. I nearly
  rejected a correct board on a broken test.

  ⚑ NO PER-BOARD SHARD IS WRITTEN, unlike the sibling shelves. The app fetches
  /reference-atlas-<shelf>-k<k>.json and nothing else, so the copy under public/euhalf/ was 77 MB of disk
  for nothing and public/ is already 2 GB with Marek asking about space. Provenance stays in
  tools/ctrnact-oracle/run-*/ and public/euhalf/manifest.json records what was built.

  SIZES. k<=4 is 65 entries and rides the eager atlas (0.1 MB). k=5..14 are ten lazy slices, 110 MB of
  JSON that gzips to about 7 — so what laziness defers is PARSING, not bandwidth. Fetched only when the
  k chip itself is chosen: unlike tri45 (7.4 MB, pulled by picking the class) and Penrose (pulled by
  picking the board), ten slices at this size must not come down for either. Deep links resolve one shard
  through the /play RESOLVERS table, which exists for exactly this.

  GUARD: lib/tilings/eu-half.test.ts, 11 checks. Metadata against the shards, the k accounting, the tile
  being a genuine HALF (its angles doubled give the n-gon's plus 180 per midpoint end of the cut), the
  side lengths against 1/sin(pi/n), 1/tan(pi/n) and cot(pi/2n), BOTH classification filters re-derived,
  the folder placement, and the shipped eager bytes re-certified for shape and area. Writing it caught my
  own halves-add-back formula being wrong: a bisected corner adds nothing, only a midpoint end adds 180.

  ⚑ AND THE SHELF SURFACED A PRE-EXISTING BUG. Selecting k=12 painted "failed to load k=12" beside the
  count and 404'd. Not the new shards — the BASE Euclidean loader fires for any k >= 8, and the base
  Ctrnact shards are k = 8, 9, 10 only, so every higher k has always 404'd. It was unreachable while
  nothing offered a Euclidean chip above 10; these boards go to k=14, which is how it showed up. Bounded
  to HIGHER_K, the constant that already lists the shards that exist.

FINAL STATE
  27,728 tilings on four boards, four folders, one per tile shape. Full suite 3 failures of 2,435, all
  three pre-existing and outside this work (star-general-path is the documented 60 s timeout,
  exact-star-polygon is ring arithmetic, paramCell is the mixed atlas). Build clean, tsc clean,
  make check-regular PASS byte-identical.
